x6 sizes).\n\nLet me write the JSON.\n\nAlso note: \"Page 4 of 6\", \"Page 5 of 6\", \"Page 6 of 6\" → page_total 6.\n\nScheme: from number \"-A-\" → \"A\".\n\nLet me now also decide whether to include certificate at all — yes.\n\nOne more consideration: the fragment header shows certificate number with \"revision 4\". Should certificate.number be \"R137/2012-A-NL1-20.07\" and revision noted? I'll add revision_history entry {\"revision\": \"4\", \"changes\": \"This revision replaces the previous version.\"} — date omitted.\n\nNow, model_family.family_name: I'll use null. Or maybe \"Q.Sonic max / Q.Sonic max6\". Since both are explicit family names, and schema allows only one family_name string, I'll set null and let models carry it. Hmm, actually maybe set family_name to \"Q.Sonic\"? Not stated as family name. null it is.\n\ncertified_type.description: \"Family of ultrasonic gas meters (Q.Sonic max and Q.Sonic max6), diameter sizes 3\\\"–36\\\" (DN80–DN900), for bi-directional flow measurement.\" The bi-directional statement: \"Bi-directional flow measurement — During conformity assessment it is sufficient to verify a bi-directional meter only in one direction.\" OK grounded.\n\nAlso \"Instruments\" is the term used (\"family of instruments\"). Category \"Gas meter\" is fine given R137 and \"ultrasonic gas meter\" mentions.\n\nLet me now think about whether to include v_t footnote [2] on Q.Sonic max values: Table 3 header: V_t^{[1],[2]} yes.\n\nWrite final JSON.\n\nFor footnote markers format — the markdown uses \"[1]\" style. Rule 7 example used \"1)\". I'll use \"[1]\" as it appears.\n\nCompact JSON but valid. Let me draft.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"certificate\": {\n    \"number\": \"R137/2012-A-NL1-20.07\",\n    \"scheme\": \"A\",\n    \"project_number\": \"3684021\",\n    \"page_total\": 6,\n    \"member_state\": \"The Netherlands\"\n  },\n  \"issuing_authority\": {\n    \"name\": \"NMi\"\n  },\n  \"certified_type\": {\n    \"category\": \"Gas meter\",\n    \"type_designations\": [\"Q.Sonic max\", \"Q.Sonic max6\"],\n    \"description\": \"Ultrasonic gas meters. Table 3: general characteristics of the Q.Sonic max family (4\\\"–36\\\" / DN100–DN900). Table 4: general characteristics of the Q.Sonic max6 family (3\\\"–36\\\" / DN80–DN900). Corresponding flow rates: Q = V · 1/4 · π · ID² · 3600 = 900 · π · ID² · V (Q in m³/h, V in m/s, ID in m).\"\n  },\n  ...\n}\n```\n\nHmm, wait — should I include flow_rate formula in description AND type_level? I'll put formula as type_level characteristic \"flow_rate_calculation\" and keep description shorter.\n\ntype_level:\n```json\n\"type_level\": {\n  \"flow_rate_formula\": {\"value\": \"Q = 900 · π · ID² · V\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null},\n  \"flow_rate_formula_variables\": ... no, skip.\n  \"minimum_outlet_straight_pipe_length\": {\"value\": {\"min\": 3, \"max\": null}, \"unit_symbol\": \"D\"},\n  \"inlet_pipe_nominal_diameter_max_tolerance\": {\"value\": 3, \"unit_symbol\": \"%\"},\n  \"temperature_sensor_distance_from_meter_outlet\": {\"value\": {\"min\": 2, \"max\": 5}, \"unit_symbol\": \"D\"}\n}\n```\n\nThe characteristic values per rule 2: objects with value/unit_symbol/unit_id/footnote_markers. So each type_level entry: {\"value\": ..., \"unit_symbol\": ..., \"unit_id\": null}. Should I include unit_id everywhere? Rule 3: \"Leave unit_id: null unless certain\". I'll include unit_id: null in each value object for schema conformance. Actually rule 2 shape includes unit_id. I'll include it.\n\nAlso add \"bidirectional_meter_verification\": {\"value\": \"verification in one direction is sufficient during conformity assessment\"}. Hmm — I'll include as it's a notable characteristic. Or skip. I'll include; it's grounded.\n\nmodel_level: five attributes. Let me write them out.\n\nModels list order — I'll interleave? No: first all Q.Sonic max (14), then Q.Sonic max6 (15).\n\nattribute: \"min_internal_bore\" unit \"mm\":\nvalues:\n{\"model\":\"Q.Sonic max DN100\",\"value\":90},